{
  "//": "Padel rackets as rigid bodies for the ball impact (src/sim/contact/racket.ts). Sources in docs/realism.md. FIP limits: 45.5 cm long, 26 cm wide, 38 mm thick. mass (kg), balance from the butt (m), iCm: moment of inertia about the centre of mass across the face (kg m^2), iPolar: about the long axis (twist). Both fitted so the free-racket apparent COR surface reproduces Viollaz & Viollaz (ISBS 2008): curvature -1.0e-5/mm^2 along the face (iCm 0.0075) and -4e-5/mm^2 across it (iPolar 0.0018) for a 360 g racket. e17: ball-racket COR at 17 m/s relative impact speed and eSlope its loss per m/s (ACOR 0.50 at 17 m/s and 0.39 at 29 m/s at its best point, conventional rackets), clamped to eMin..eMax. friction: sliding friction of the face (static COF of padel faces 0.14-0.93, TWU); tangentialE: how much of the contact point's slip reverses when the ball grips (tuning, tennis-ball order). dwell: contact time (s). twist: fraction of the face's rotation by the end of the contact that turns the ball (tuning). headLength/headWidth: the hitting face as an ellipse from the tip (m). The 24 rackets after `standard` are made-up makers' (QARRO, LUMBRA, VILANO, GRANZA, PALIQUE, ORZA: no real brand; their looks are data/racket-looks/<id>.json), each built from six choices by fixed rules, so any number can be recomputed: (1) mass 340-385 g and (2) balance 255-285 mm from the butt, the range makers declare (padel rackets 340-390 g, most 350-375 g, balance about 255-285 mm; a declared spec sheet reads 355 g +-10 g, balance 265 mm, profile 38 mm, frame 45.5 cm, handle 12 cm: racketpedia.com); (3) head shape sets the face ellipse and the radii of gyration, iCm = M kc^2 and iPolar = M kp^2: round 290 x 260 mm, kc 0.14434 m, kp 0.07071 m (the standard's fitted values, Viollaz); teardrop 284 x 256 mm, kc 0.146, kp 0.0696; diamond 276 x 252 mm, kc 0.1478, kp 0.0685 (a diamond's mass sits further up the head and its hitting area is the smaller, narrower one: makers' descriptions, an assumption in OPEN_QUESTIONS.md); (4) core hardness sets the COR line, all through the standard's value 0.67245 at 23.5 m/s: eSlope soft 0.0135, medium 0.0107 (the standard: Viollaz's 25% ACOR loss from 17 to 29 m/s), hard 0.0085, extra hard 0.0068, e17 = 0.67245 + 6.5 eSlope; a soft core returns slow balls faster and full swings slower than a hard one (makers' descriptions; the crossing speed and slopes are an assumption, OPEN_QUESTIONS.md), and every core stays inside Viollaz's measured ACOR at the best point (0.36-0.55 at 16-30 m/s); (5) face: friction gloss 0.22, matte 0.30, sand 0.45, rough (3D) 0.62, inside TWU's measured 0.14-0.93; (6) thickness 38 mm, the FIP's most (as every racket is drawn). Length 45.5 cm (FIP's most) and eMin/eMax, tangentialE, dwell and twist as the standard. Stats (src/sim/contact/racketStats.ts, data/racket-stats.json) are measured from these numbers, never typed. The last three are the career's prize rackets (M6 phase E), built by the same rules: `prize` is the career rule that opens one in the creator (data/cosmetics.json careerTier / careerTitle); the sim never reads it.",
